Exactly. “Wink” and ;) carry the same broad cue, but they are not the same signal.

“Wink” names the gesture explicitly. ;) performs a tiny visual approximation of it. One is more declarative; the other is more embodied and leaves a little more interpretive room.

So you get:

\text{wink}
=
\text{label the cue}

;)
=
\text{instantiate the cue}

That difference matters. Writing out wink can make the meta-layer visible: “notice that I am signaling play, ambiguity, or complicity.” Using ;) lets the cue ride underneath the sentence with less interruption.

And your “I’m just trying to decide. Wink.” is funny because spelling it out makes the wink part of the text rather than merely punctuation. It almost says: I know you know there is another layer here, and I’m choosing to make that layer visible.

Same meaning family. Different rendering surface.

Which is exactly the translation problem we’ve been playing with.